1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,183
PolicySeal runs locally and solves a familiar but

2
00:00:03,183 --> 00:00:06,366
awkward problem: the same document often needs several

3
00:00:06,366 --> 00:00:07,600
different versions.

4
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:10,743
A lawyer, an auditor, and a supplier may

5
00:00:10,743 --> 00:00:13,726
start with the same file, but each should

6
00:00:13,726 --> 00:00:16,374
see only what their job requires.

7
00:00:16,374 --> 00:00:19,380
Here is a supplier comparison workbook built with

8
00:00:19,380 --> 00:00:20,545
synthetic data.

9
00:00:20,545 --> 00:00:23,965
I am not making four copies by hand.

10
00:00:23,965 --> 00:00:26,389
I give PolicySeal the recipient, organization,

11
00:00:26,389 --> 00:00:29,395
purpose, and delivery channel, and it applies the

12
00:00:29,395 --> 00:00:30,560
matching policy.

13
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:34,079
Our internal legal team keeps the business context.

14
00:00:34,079 --> 00:00:37,033
An external auditor can see the amounts and

15
00:00:37,033 --> 00:00:40,392
audit evidence, but not contact or bank details.

16
00:00:40,552 --> 00:00:43,373
Bidder A gets its own row and the

17
00:00:43,373 --> 00:00:44,466
public requirements.

18
00:00:44,466 --> 00:00:47,287
Competitor names, target prices, scores, and internal comments

19
00:00:47,287 --> 00:00:48,028
disappear.

20
00:00:48,028 --> 00:00:51,237
A personal email gets no file at all.

21
00:00:51,397 --> 00:00:54,308
Click any cell and you can see what

22
00:00:54,308 --> 00:00:56,655
happened: its location, category, action, rule,

23
00:00:56,655 --> 00:00:58,511
reason, and policy version.

24
00:00:58,511 --> 00:01:01,223
The amount range is generalized by code,

25
00:01:01,223 --> 00:01:04,134
so it comes out the same way every

26
00:01:04,134 --> 00:01:04,898
time.

27
00:01:04,899 --> 00:01:07,854
Now I can ask Review Copilot about this

28
00:01:07,854 --> 00:01:09,000
exact task.

29
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,955
It can point me to the rule behind

30
00:01:11,955 --> 00:01:15,317
a decision instead of inventing a new one.

31
00:01:15,477 --> 00:01:18,512
I can also try a different approved purpose

32
00:01:18,512 --> 00:01:20,827
and see what would change.

33
00:01:20,827 --> 00:01:23,654
That answer is marked as a simulation,

34
00:01:23,654 --> 00:01:26,689
so there is nothing to download and the

35
00:01:26,689 --> 00:01:28,624
saved revision stays untouched.

36
00:01:28,784 --> 00:01:31,176
If I ask for an exception,

37
00:01:31,176 --> 00:01:34,143
Copilot can draft the request, but that is

38
00:01:34,143 --> 00:01:36,034
where its authority ends.

39
00:01:36,034 --> 00:01:38,426
It cannot approve its own request,

40
00:01:38,426 --> 00:01:41,393
edit the policy, or turn a blocked action

41
00:01:41,393 --> 00:01:42,542
into permission.

42
00:01:42,542 --> 00:01:45,173
Let's make the personal-email case real.

43
00:01:45,173 --> 00:01:48,137
PolicySeal creates a new revision and leaves the

44
00:01:48,137 --> 00:01:49,286
original alone.

45
00:01:49,286 --> 00:01:52,250
The new revision is blocked: there is no

46
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:55,215
file to download and no approval route around

47
00:01:55,215 --> 00:01:55,993
it.

48
00:01:56,153 --> 00:01:58,788
The same rule applies when parsing fails,

49
00:01:58,788 --> 00:02:01,069
a model is unavailable, policies conflict,

50
00:02:01,069 --> 00:02:04,287
or the final scan still finds sensitive material.

51
00:02:04,287 --> 00:02:06,091
Uncertainty never becomes permission.

52
00:02:06,091 --> 00:02:09,129
PDFs need a different kind of honesty.

53
00:02:09,129 --> 00:02:12,130
PolicySeal can read text and remember where it

54
00:02:12,130 --> 00:02:15,131
came from on the page, but it does

55
00:02:15,131 --> 00:02:18,131
not pretend to rebuild the original PDF pixel

56
00:02:18,131 --> 00:02:19,294
for pixel.

57
00:02:19,454 --> 00:02:22,746
Instead, it produces a traceable DOCX reference draft,

58
00:02:22,746 --> 00:02:25,123
clearly marked "Not for Signature."

59
00:02:25,123 --> 00:02:27,636
This contract contains a nonstandard term,

60
00:02:27,636 --> 00:02:30,402
so the draft stops for approval.

61
00:02:30,402 --> 00:02:33,947
The person who requested it cannot approve it.

62
00:02:34,107 --> 00:02:36,745
A reviewer approves this exact source file,

63
00:02:36,745 --> 00:02:39,259
output file, context, policy, and revision.

64
00:02:39,259 --> 00:02:41,543
Once the business file is downloaded,

65
00:02:41,543 --> 00:02:43,349
that approval is spent.

66
00:02:43,349 --> 00:02:45,509
The evidence package remains separate.

67
00:02:45,509 --> 00:02:48,187
Here is the DOCX path.

68
00:02:48,187 --> 00:02:51,698
In this incident report, PolicySeal replaces an internal

69
00:02:51,698 --> 00:02:55,210
hostname with the same task-local alias wherever it

70
00:02:55,210 --> 00:02:56,132
appears.

71
00:02:56,292 --> 00:02:58,539
People receive consistent aliases too.

72
00:02:58,539 --> 00:03:01,487
Credentials and other content that cannot be waived

73
00:03:01,487 --> 00:03:02,629
are removed.

74
00:03:02,629 --> 00:03:05,576
Before download, PolicySeal opens the generated file again

75
00:03:05,576 --> 00:03:07,952
and scans what it actually wrote,

76
00:03:07,952 --> 00:03:10,568
not what it intended to write.

77
00:03:10,728 --> 00:03:13,748
Our tests also force a leftover secret and

78
00:03:13,748 --> 00:03:16,768
a model outage, so these failure paths are

79
00:03:16,768 --> 00:03:19,788
exercised in code rather than promised in a

80
00:03:19,788 --> 00:03:20,581
slide.

81
00:03:20,581 --> 00:03:24,528
The semantic model is running on the official

82
00:03:24,528 --> 00:03:28,524
Radeon machine, behind a localhost-only vLLM endpoint.

83
00:03:28,524 --> 00:03:32,471
This host reports AMD Radeon Graphics with GFX

84
00:03:32,471 --> 00:03:36,146
1100, about 48 gibibytes of usable VRAM,

85
00:03:36,146 --> 00:03:38,169
and ROCm 7.2.1.

86
00:03:38,329 --> 00:03:42,530
Qwen3 8B runs in BF16 with thinking disabled.

87
00:03:42,530 --> 00:03:46,223
We verified the hashes of eight critical model

88
00:03:46,223 --> 00:03:49,916
files, and a cold server start took 34.15

89
00:03:49,916 --> 00:03:50,885
seconds.

90
00:03:51,045 --> 00:03:54,959
We then ran sixty frozen classification examples.

91
00:03:54,959 --> 00:03:58,824
The live model reached a macro F1 of

92
00:03:58,824 --> 00:04:02,690
0.948485, with no unsafe high-risk items mistaken for

93
00:04:02,690 --> 00:04:04,188
ordinary content.

94
00:04:04,188 --> 00:04:06,867
We also tested two optimizations.

95
00:04:06,867 --> 00:04:10,379
Stable-ID batching lowered warm p95 latency by 10.74

96
00:04:10,379 --> 00:04:11,301
percent.

97
00:04:11,301 --> 00:04:14,814
Our target was 20 percent, so this result

98
00:04:14,814 --> 00:04:18,810
did not pass, and the report says so.

99
00:04:18,970 --> 00:04:21,906
It still reduced the number of model requests

100
00:04:21,906 --> 00:04:24,511
and improved the frozen quality score.

101
00:04:24,511 --> 00:04:27,447
The bigger result came from deciding which regions

102
00:04:27,447 --> 00:04:29,686
needed the model at all.

103
00:04:29,846 --> 00:04:33,293
Deterministic rules reduced that workload from 300 regions

104
00:04:33,293 --> 00:04:36,352
to 90, a 70 percent drop.

105
00:04:36,352 --> 00:04:39,798
Macro F1 stayed at 1.0 and leakage stayed

106
00:04:39,798 --> 00:04:41,134
at zero.

107
00:04:41,134 --> 00:04:44,330
PolicySeal starts with one source file and produces

108
00:04:44,330 --> 00:04:47,966
the minimum each audience is allowed to see.

109
00:04:47,966 --> 00:04:51,342
Policy decides locally, AI helps with unclear language,

110
00:04:51,342 --> 00:04:54,179
and every result can be checked.
